From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 12:55:11 +0200
Subject: MIPS: dts: intel-mips: give the 512 MiB boards their full memory back

Both GRX550 boards carry 512 MiB and declare 256. Take the cap out.

The cap was never a statement about the memory. "MIPS: dts: intel-mips:
cap the FRITZ!Box 7590 at the uncached window" put it in as a
coherent-DMA-correctness measure: above PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M the KSEG1
alias of a page is a register address, and with no way to steer the page
allocator away from those pages, the only lever left was to stop
declaring them. It cost the 7590 half its RAM to keep one class of
allocation honest, and the 7580 inherited the same node for the same
reason.

That lever is no longer the only one. ZONE_DMA is now sized to the
uncached window and dma-direct allocates every uncached coherent buffer
from it, so the bound is enforced where the allocation happens instead
of by the width of a memory node. The two changes hold exactly the same
line; this one just stops charging the board 256 MiB for it.

Recovering the width was parked rather than abandoned, and what parked
it was a boot failure that turned out not to be about memory at all.
With the zones split, ordinary GFP_KERNEL pages start coming from above
0x30000000, and the first module then loaded into a reserved-instruction
exception. The defect is in OpenWrt's generic hack patch 301-02, which
replaces the module allocator with a linear-map one so that modules can
be built -mno-long-calls: it places module text with no jal-region
constraint, judges reachability against a 512 MiB window when a jal
reaches 256, and writes PLT trampolines into a kmalloc buffer that
nothing ever flushes to the I-cache. All three are fixed on the OpenWrt
side. Nothing in this kernel was implicated, which is why nothing else
here changes.

Tested on a FRITZ!Box 7590: MemTotal reports the full width, the zones
split at 0x30000000, modules load, both QCA9984 radios come up across a
down/up cycle, and a tmpfs large enough to force allocations above the
window round-trips its contents intact -- with no reserved-instruction
exception, no data bus error and no refusal from the
arch_dma_set_uncached() guard.

The shm-rng-seed reservation goes back to being real -- 0x3dfb0000 falls
inside the node again -- so the note above reserved-memory says which
regions the uncached bound applies to rather than claiming all of them
are below it. And the memory node now carries the container coupling
that the width depends on: it is safe only while no second kernel record
starts the boot loader that declares 256 MiB and fences the fabric off
above it.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7580.dts b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7580.dts
index 57e5c4e7daaf..ea3a1b4ca81d 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7580.dts
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7580.dts
@@ -31,13 +31,24 @@ avm-hw-revision {
 	};
 
 	/*
-	 * Capped at the top of the uncached window (PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M):
-	 * above it the KSEG1 alias of a page is a register address, so no
-	 * coherent allocation may land there.
+	 * 512 MiB DDR at PHYS_OFFSET, the full part -- the same shape as the
+	 * FRITZ!Box 7590, whose node carries the derivation.
+	 *
+	 * Only the first half has an uncached alias: above
+	 * PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M the KSEG1 alias of a page is a register
+	 * address rather than DRAM. ZONE_DMA is sized to that window and
+	 * dma-direct takes every uncached coherent allocation from there, so
+	 * the bound is enforced by the allocator rather than by leaving half
+	 * the board undeclared. Cached access is good across the whole node.
+	 *
+	 * Single-kernel containers only, for the same reason as on the 7590:
+	 * this board has no boot loader blob of its own either, and the one
+	 * it would have to borrow declares 256 MiB and fences the fabric off
+	 * above that -- over exactly the half this node adds.
 	 */
 	memory@20000000 {
 		device_type = "memory";
-		reg = <0x20000000 0x10000000>;
+		reg = <0x20000000 0x20000000>;
 	};
 
 	/*
@@ -66,10 +77,12 @@ chosen {
 	};
 
 	/*
-	 * Every region below must stay under PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M; the
-	 * uncached alias above that address reaches registers, not DRAM. The
-	 * addresses are the vendor's, stated once for the whole GRX550
-	 * family.
+	 * Any region below that a device reaches through the uncached alias
+	 * must stay under PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M; above that address the
+	 * alias reaches registers, not DRAM. The seed buffer at the end is
+	 * the one that sits higher, and may, because it is no-map and this
+	 * kernel never reaches it at all. The addresses are the vendor's,
+	 * stated once for the whole GRX550 family.
 	 */
 	reserved-memory {
 		#address-cells = <1>;
diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts
index 0125c91f084f..766949961be1 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts
@@ -31,13 +31,28 @@ avm-hw-revision {
 	};
 
 	/*
-	 * Capped at the top of the uncached window (PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M):
-	 * above it the KSEG1 alias of a page is a register address, so no
-	 * coherent allocation may land there.
+	 * 512 MiB DDR at PHYS_OFFSET, the full part.
+	 *
+	 * Only the first half of it has an uncached alias: above
+	 * PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M the KSEG1 alias of a page is a register
+	 * address rather than DRAM. That bound has not moved. What moved is
+	 * where it is enforced -- ZONE_DMA is sized to the window and
+	 * dma-direct takes every uncached coherent allocation from there --
+	 * so holding it no longer costs the board half its memory. Cached
+	 * access is good across the whole node; KSEG0 covers all of it.
+	 *
+	 * The width is coupled to the boot container, and the coupling is
+	 * exact rather than stylistic: it is safe only while the second
+	 * CPU's boot loader stays unstarted. That firmware declares 256 MiB
+	 * and programs a deny-all fabric region over everything above it --
+	 * precisely the half this node adds -- and the first access to that
+	 * half then faults or hangs. There is no boot loader blob for this
+	 * board, so the way it stays unstarted is to ship the kernel in a
+	 * single-kernel container, which is what the image recipe does.
 	 */
 	memory@20000000 {
 		device_type = "memory";
-		reg = <0x20000000 0x10000000>;
+		reg = <0x20000000 0x20000000>;
 	};
 
 	/*
@@ -66,8 +81,11 @@ chosen {
 	};
 
 	/*
-	 * Every region below must stay under PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M; the
-	 * uncached alias above that address reaches registers, not DRAM.
+	 * Any region below that a device reaches through the uncached alias
+	 * must stay under PHYS_OFFSET + SZ_256M; above that address the
+	 * alias reaches registers, not DRAM. The seed buffer at the end is
+	 * the one that sits higher, and may, because it is no-map and this
+	 * kernel never reaches it at all.
 	 */
 	reserved-memory {
 		#address-cells = <1>;
